#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152916} :activity/id #uuid "aaea3aa0-57fb-51ad-9a03-12cce1e87f71" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004 :topic/id #uuid "d28e701c-f952-587a-ba2a-6c1d3807d35a" :topic/math-academy-id 2641} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153445 :tutorial/content "Suppose $a$ is an integer, and ${\\color{red}{n}}$ is a positive integer. As we've seen, the *division algorithm* states that there exists a unique integer $q$ and a unique positive integer ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ such that\n\n$$\na = q{\\color{red}{n}}+{\\color{blue}{r}}, \\qquad 0\\leq {\\color{blue}{r}} < {\\color{red}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWhen two integers $a$ and $b$ have the *same remainder* ${\\color{blue}{r}}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{n}},$ we say they are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol n,$ and we write\n\n$$\na\\equiv b\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nThe number $n$ is called the **modulus**.\n\nTo demonstrate this concretely, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n1, \\qquad 6, \\qquad 11, \\qquad 16\n$$\n\nThese numbers are **congruent modulo** $\\boldsymbol 5$ because they each have the same remainder $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =0\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 6 & =1\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 11 & =2\\cdot 5+1 \\\\ 16 & =3\\cdot 5+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 1 & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 1 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 6 & ≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 6 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 11 & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, consider the following list of numbers:\n\n$$\n2, \\qquad 7, \\qquad 12, \\qquad 17\n$$\n\nThese numbers are *also* congruent modulo $5$ because they each have a remainder of $\\color{blue}{2}$ when divided by ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =0\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 7 & =1\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 5+2 \\\\ 17 & =3\\cdot 5+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 2 & ≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 2 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 7 & ≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 7 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 12 & ≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo visualize congruence modulo $5$ more generally, let's write the sequence $0, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\ldots$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $5$ numbers.\n\n![](images/24/24c9af7d731f0be479f36367ce2e1deb0d2d691ffa575cfa843a1ca1742d2698.png)\n\nEach \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $5.$ Every number on a given spoke is congruent modulo $5$ to every other number on that spoke." :tutorial/id #uuid "079c66bc-ad87-56ed-85da-3ad13ba65bd5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9010 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "75bff31b-8ddd-5ea9-8a7e-ab19757aeb37" :step/math-academy-id 137420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153471 :tutorial/content "In general, two integers $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b).$ \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ because $5\\,\\mid\\, (6-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-1)\\div 5 = 5\\div 5 = 1\n$$\n\n- $-2\\equiv -8\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$ because $6\\,\\mid\\, \\left((-2)-(-8)\\right).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n\\left((-2)-(-8)\\right)\\div 6 = 6\\div 6 = 1\n$$\n\nHowever, the following numbers are not congruent with respect to the given modulus:\n\n- $6\\not\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because $4\\,\\not\\mid\\, (6-1).$ Note that $(6-1) = 5,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- $0\\not\\equiv 13\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,12)$ because $12\\,\\not\\mid\\, (0-13).$ Note that $(0-13) = -13,$ which is not divisible by $12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "30bcacad-ba7e-5d26-840a-8d0c84a74224" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9122 :tutorial/title "Determining Congruence Using Divisibility"} :step/id #uuid "26676e74-1311-5b88-b034-afe0442bd091" :step/math-academy-id 137421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151832 :question/id #uuid "e64a4c4d-502b-508e-b7af-b4012cd16617" :question/math-academy-id "e-14578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409444}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"} :step/id #uuid "4ac6527c-e05c-5495-999a-f85278c133e4" :step/math-academy-id 137422 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151833 :question/id #uuid "041ab8f3-ced5-5a79-b639-190850c06891" :question/math-academy-id "e-14579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409441}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"} :step/id #uuid "be736307-e668-5fd7-8bb7-218e7e6eece1" :step/math-academy-id 137425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151835 :question/id #uuid "afcb17e2-d38b-5ebc-b135-f8cf68a601ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-14581"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409442}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"} :step/id #uuid "8edeecd2-3305-5e12-9584-78b353b073d5" :step/math-academy-id 137428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153449 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've been using the fact that $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $(a-b){:}$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)\n$$\n\nTo prove this result, first note that if $a$ and $b$ have the same remainder $r$ when divided by $n,$ then by the division algorithm\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =pn+r \\\\ b & =qn+r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some integers $p$ and $q.$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\n(a-b) = (p-q)n.\n$$\n\nNow, if we denote $k=p-q\\in\\mathbb Z,$ then\n\n$$\n(a-b) = kn.\n$$\n\nSo, $(a-b)$ is an integer multiple of $n,$ which means that $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n.$ \n\nConversely, assume that $n \\mid (a-b)$. Then there is an integer $k$ such that $a-b= nk.$ \n\nNow, by Euclid's division algorithm, there exist unique integers $m$ and $r\\in \\{0, 1, \\dots n-1\\}$ such that $b={\\color{blue} n}m+\\color{red}r.$ Note that this means that $b \\,  \\cong \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b=nk & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=b+nk \\\\  & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=(nm+r)+nk \\\\  & \\,\\,⟹\\,\\,a=n(m+k)+r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,  $a \\, \\equiv \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n)$ and $b \\,  \\equiv \\, r \\, \\,   (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n),$ so $a \\, \\equiv \\, b  \\, \\,  (\\textrm{mod}  \\, n).$\n\nThis proves that if  $n$ divides $(a-b)$ then $a$ is congruent to $b$ modulo $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5201f644-599c-5365-8b1d-acb9b2a8bc13" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9015 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Divisibility Property"} :step/id #uuid "a72a3671-4f8b-50a5-a49c-4ed6899fb537" :step/math-academy-id 137431}] :activity/title "Modular Congruence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152336}]}
